{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Session 1 — From Poisson to Galerkin\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Finite differences, weak formulation, projection, and the motivation for finite elements\"\n",
        "jupyter: python3\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "By the end of this session, students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- explain the difference between a strong and a weak formulation;\n",
        "- derive a finite-difference approximation of the Poisson equation;\n",
        "- explain that finite differences approximate differential operators directly;\n",
        "- derive the weak formulation of the Poisson problem;\n",
        "- construct a finite-dimensional Galerkin approximation;\n",
        "- interpret Galerkin approximation as an orthogonal projection;\n",
        "- explain the best-approximation property for the Poisson problem;\n",
        "- distinguish global, domain-specific approximation spaces from mesh-based finite-element spaces.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Schedule\n",
        "\n",
        "| Time | Topic |\n",
        "|---:|---|\n",
        "| 0–10 min | Poisson equation and model problem |\n",
        "| 10–25 min | Finite differences: direct approximation of derivatives |\n",
        "| 25–45 min | Strong form → weak form |\n",
        "| 45–58 min | Galerkin approximation |\n",
        "| 58–70 min | Global basis functions on a square |\n",
        "| 70–82 min | Galerkin orthogonality and projection |\n",
        "| 82–90 min | Why finite elements? |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Model problem\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the Poisson equation\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\Delta u = f\n",
        "\\qquad \\text{in } \\Omega,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u = 0\n",
        "\\qquad \\text{on } \\partial\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Possible physical interpretations include:\n",
        "\n",
        "- steady heat conduction;\n",
        "- electrostatic potential;\n",
        "- deflection of a membrane.\n",
        "\n",
        "The objective of the course is to construct a numerical approximation of \\(u\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# A first discretization: finite differences\n",
        "\n",
        "Before introducing finite elements, consider the one-dimensional problem\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-u''(x)=f(x),\n",
        "\\qquad x\\in(0,1),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u(0)=u(1)=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Introduce a regular grid\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_i=ih,\n",
        "\\qquad i=0,\\ldots,N.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using Taylor expansions around \\(x_i\\),\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u(x_i+h)\n",
        "=\n",
        "u(x_i)\n",
        "+\n",
        "h u'(x_i)\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\frac{h^2}{2}u''(x_i)\n",
        "+\n",
        "O(h^3),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u(x_i-h)\n",
        "=\n",
        "u(x_i)\n",
        "-\n",
        "h u'(x_i)\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\frac{h^2}{2}u''(x_i)\n",
        "+\n",
        "O(h^3).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Adding the two expressions gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u''(x_i)\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "u_{i-1}-2u_i+u_{i+1}\n",
        "}{h^2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "-u_{i-1}+2u_i-u_{i+1}\n",
        "}{h^2}\n",
        "=\n",
        "f_i.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For the interior unknowns this gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{1}{h^2}\n",
        "\\begin{pmatrix}\n",
        "2 & -1 & & \\\\\n",
        "-1 & 2 & -1 & \\\\\n",
        "& \\ddots & \\ddots & \\ddots \\\\\n",
        "& & -1 & 2\n",
        "\\end{pmatrix}\n",
        "\\begin{pmatrix}\n",
        "u_1\\\\\n",
        "u_2\\\\\n",
        "\\vdots\\\\\n",
        "u_{N-1}\n",
        "\\end{pmatrix}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{pmatrix}\n",
        "f_1\\\\\n",
        "f_2\\\\\n",
        "\\vdots\\\\\n",
        "f_{N-1}\n",
        "\\end{pmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "Finite differences proceed directly from the differential equation:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\text{PDE}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{approximation of derivatives at grid points}.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The unknowns \\(u_i\\) approximate point values\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_i \\approx u(x_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## The two-dimensional stencil\n",
        "\n",
        "On a Cartesian grid,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\Delta u\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2}\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial y^2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a regular square grid with spacing \\(h\\),\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\Delta u_{ij}\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "4u_{ij}\n",
        "-u_{i-1,j}\n",
        "-u_{i+1,j}\n",
        "-u_{i,j-1}\n",
        "-u_{i,j+1}\n",
        "}{h^2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This corresponds to the classical five-point stencil:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "          -1\n",
        "           |\n",
        "      -1   4   -1\n",
        "           |\n",
        "          -1\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This method is extremely natural on a rectangular domain with a Cartesian grid.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Finite-difference example\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\Delta u = 1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "on the unit square\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Omega=(0,1)^2,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u=0\n",
        "\\qquad \\text{on } \\partial\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We solve the discrete system numerically using a finite-difference approximation on a Cartesian grid."
      ],
      "id": "bcb5a631"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fd-2d\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Finite-difference solution of $-\\Delta u=1$ on the unit square.\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "# Number of intervals in each direction\n",
        "N = 10\n",
        "h = 1 / N\n",
        "\n",
        "# Complete grid, INCLUDING the boundary\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 1, N+1)\n",
        "y = np.linspace(0, 1, N+1)\n",
        "X, Y = np.meshgrid(x, y)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Number of interior points in one direction\n",
        "n = N - 1\n",
        "\n",
        "# 1D finite-difference matrix\n",
        "T = (\n",
        "    2*np.eye(n)\n",
        "    - np.eye(n, k=1)\n",
        "    - np.eye(n, k=-1)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "I = np.eye(n)\n",
        "\n",
        "# 2D five-point finite-difference operator\n",
        "A = (1 / h**2) * (\n",
        "    np.kron(I, T)\n",
        "    + np.kron(T, I)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# f = 1 at the interior nodes\n",
        "f = np.ones(n*n)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Solve for interior nodal values\n",
        "u = np.linalg.solve(A, f)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Complete solution, including u = 0 on the boundary\n",
        "U = np.zeros((N+1, N+1))\n",
        "\n",
        "U[1:-1, 1:-1] = u.reshape((n, n))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot the solution\n",
        "plt.contourf(X, Y, U, levels=20)\n",
        "plt.colorbar(label=\"$u_h$\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot Cartesian grid\n",
        "for xi in x:\n",
        "    plt.plot([xi, xi], [0, 1], linewidth=0.5)\n",
        "\n",
        "for yi in y:\n",
        "    plt.plot([0, 1], [yi, yi], linewidth=0.5)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot all grid nodes\n",
        "plt.plot(X.ravel(), Y.ravel(), \"o\", markersize=2)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"$x$\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"$y$\")\n",
        "plt.gca().set_aspect(\"equal\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fd-2d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fd-sparsity\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Sparsity pattern of the finite-difference matrix.\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure()\n",
        "plt.spy(A, markersize=2)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Column\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Row\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Sparsity pattern of $A$\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fd-sparsity",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Question\n",
        "\n",
        "What happens when:\n",
        "\n",
        "- the boundary is curved?\n",
        "- the geometry contains a hole?\n",
        "- the domain is irregular?\n",
        "- the mesh is not Cartesian?\n",
        "\n",
        "This motivates looking for a formulation that is less tied to direct pointwise approximations of the differential operator.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# From strong to weak formulation\n",
        "\n",
        "Return to the Poisson equation\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\Delta u=f\n",
        "\\qquad\\text{in }\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let \\(v\\) be a test function satisfying\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "v=0\n",
        "\\qquad\\text{on }\\partial\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Multiply the equation by \\(v\\) and integrate:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\int_\\Omega v\\,\\Delta u\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega fv\\,d\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using Green's identity,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "\\nabla u\\cdot\\nabla v\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\n",
        "v\\,\\nabla u\\cdot n\\,d\\Gamma\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega fv\\,d\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "v=0\n",
        "\\qquad\\text{on }\\partial\\Omega,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the boundary term vanishes and we obtain\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "\\nabla u\\cdot\\nabla v\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega fv\\,d\\Omega.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(u,v)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "\\nabla u\\cdot\\nabla v\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(v)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega fv\\,d\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The weak problem is therefore:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\text{Find }u\\in V\n",
        "\\text{ such that }\n",
        "a(u,v)=\\ell(v)\n",
        "\\quad\n",
        "\\forall v\\in V,\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=H_0^1(\\Omega).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why is this useful?\n",
        "\n",
        "The strong equation involves second derivatives of \\(u\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "The weak formulation involves only first derivatives:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\nabla u.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus the regularity required of the approximation has been reduced.\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Key point:** the weak formulation is not yet a numerical approximation. It is a reformulation of the PDE.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Galerkin approximation\n",
        "\n",
        "The space \\(V\\) is infinite-dimensional.\n",
        "\n",
        "Choose a finite-dimensional subspace\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V_N\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{span}\n",
        "\\{\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_N\\}\n",
        "\\subset V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Seek\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_N\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\n",
        "U_j\\phi_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The Galerkin method imposes the weak equation only for test functions belonging to the same finite-dimensional space:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(u_N,v_N)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\ell(v_N)\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\forall v_N\\in V_N.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Choosing\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "v_N=\\phi_i,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\n",
        "a(\\phi_j,\\phi_i)U_j\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\ell(\\phi_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "K_{ij}\n",
        "=\n",
        "a(\\phi_j,\\phi_i)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_i\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\ell(\\phi_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "KU=F.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Important:** we have obtained a finite-dimensional linear system, but we have not introduced finite elements.\n",
        "\n",
        "The Galerkin method only requires the choice of a finite-dimensional approximation space \\(V_N\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Global basis functions on the square\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the Poisson problem\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\Delta u = 1\n",
        "\\qquad \\text{in } \\Omega=(0,1)^2,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u=0\n",
        "\\qquad \\text{on } \\partial\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For this simple geometry, a convenient family of admissible functions is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\phi_{mn}(x,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sin(m\\pi x)\\sin(n\\pi y),\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "m,n\\in\\mathbb{N}^*.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "These functions automatically satisfy\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi_{mn}=0\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\text{on }\\partial\\Omega.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We may therefore choose a finite-dimensional Galerkin approximation of the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_N(x,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{(m,n)\\in I_N}\n",
        "U_{mn}\\,\n",
        "\\sin(m\\pi x)\\sin(n\\pi y),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where \\(I_N\\) is a finite set of retained mode indices.\n",
        "\n",
        "These basis functions are chosen **a priori** from the geometry and boundary conditions.\n",
        "They have global support over the domain.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## One-mode Galerkin approximation\n",
        "\n",
        "We first retain only the mode\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "m=n=1,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "so that\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi_{11}(x,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sin(\\pi x)\\sin(\\pi y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We seek\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_1(x,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "U_{11}\\phi_{11}(x,y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The Galerkin equation is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "U_{11}\\,a(\\phi_{11},\\phi_{11})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\ell(\\phi_{11}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(\\phi_{11},\\phi_{11})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "|\\nabla\\phi_{11}|^2\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\pi^2}{2},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and, since \\(f=1\\),\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\phi_{11})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "\\phi_{11}\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{4}{\\pi^2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Hence\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "U_{11}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\ell(\\phi_{11})}\n",
        "{a(\\phi_{11},\\phi_{11})}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{8}{\\pi^4}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore the one-mode Galerkin approximation is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "u_1(x,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{8}{\\pi^4}\n",
        "\\sin(\\pi x)\\sin(\\pi y).\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Exact solution\n",
        "\n",
        "The exact solution can be written as the Fourier sine series\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "u(x,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{16}{\\pi^4}\n",
        "\\sum_{\\substack{m,n=1\\\\m,n\\ \\mathrm{odd}}}^{\\infty}\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "\\sin(m\\pi x)\\sin(n\\pi y)\n",
        "}{\n",
        "mn(m^2+n^2)\n",
        "}.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The first term of this exact expansion is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{16}{\\pi^4}\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "\\sin(\\pi x)\\sin(\\pi y)\n",
        "}{\n",
        "1\\cdot 1\\cdot(1^2+1^2)\n",
        "}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{8}{\\pi^4}\n",
        "\\sin(\\pi x)\\sin(\\pi y),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "which is exactly the one-mode Galerkin approximation derived above.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Comparison with the exact solution\n",
        "\n",
        "The following Python code compares the one-mode Galerkin approximation with\n",
        "a truncated Fourier-series representation of the exact solution."
      ],
      "id": "6f71ef6c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-galerkin-exact-sine\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Exact solution (truncated Fourier series) and one-mode Galerkin approximation.\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plotting grid\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 1, 151)\n",
        "y = np.linspace(0, 1, 151)\n",
        "\n",
        "X, Y = np.meshgrid(x, y)\n",
        "\n",
        "# --------------------------------\n",
        "# One-mode Galerkin approximation\n",
        "# --------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "U11 = 8 / np.pi**4\n",
        "\n",
        "U_galerkin = (\n",
        "    U11\n",
        "    * np.sin(np.pi * X)\n",
        "    * np.sin(np.pi * Y)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# --------------------------------\n",
        "# Exact solution\n",
        "# Truncated Fourier sine series\n",
        "# --------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "U_exact = np.zeros_like(X)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Number of odd modes retained\n",
        "n_modes = 30\n",
        "odd_modes = np.arange(1, 2*n_modes, 2)\n",
        "\n",
        "for m in odd_modes:\n",
        "    for n in odd_modes:\n",
        "        U_exact += (\n",
        "            16 / np.pi**4\n",
        "            * np.sin(m*np.pi*X)\n",
        "            * np.sin(n*np.pi*Y)\n",
        "            / (m*n*(m**2 + n**2))\n",
        "        )\n",
        "\n",
        "# Common scale for both plots\n",
        "vmin = 0.0\n",
        "vmax = max(U_exact.max(), U_galerkin.max())\n",
        "levels = np.linspace(vmin, vmax, 21)\n",
        "\n",
        "# --------------------------------\n",
        "# Side-by-side plots\n",
        "# --------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 4))\n",
        "\n",
        "c1 = axes[0].contourf(\n",
        "    X, Y, U_exact,\n",
        "    levels=levels\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[0].set_title(\"Exact solution\")\n",
        "axes[0].set_xlabel(\"$x$\")\n",
        "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"$y$\")\n",
        "axes[0].set_aspect(\"equal\")\n",
        "\n",
        "c2 = axes[1].contourf(\n",
        "    X, Y, U_galerkin,\n",
        "    levels=levels\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[1].set_title(\"One-mode Galerkin\")\n",
        "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"$x$\")\n",
        "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"$y$\")\n",
        "axes[1].set_aspect(\"equal\")\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.colorbar(\n",
        "    c2,\n",
        "    ax=axes,\n",
        "    label=\"$u(x,y)$\",\n",
        "    shrink=0.85\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-galerkin-exact-sine",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The one-mode approximation already captures the dominant shape of the exact\n",
        "solution, despite containing only one degree of freedom.\n",
        "\n",
        "This example is especially convenient because the chosen global basis functions\n",
        "are also eigenfunctions of the Laplacian. The Galerkin coefficients therefore\n",
        "coincide with the corresponding Fourier coefficients of the exact solution.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## One-mode approximation\n",
        "\n",
        "Take\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f=1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and use only\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi(x,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sin(\\pi x)\\sin(\\pi y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Seek\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_1=U\\phi.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Galerkin gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "U\\,a(\\phi,\\phi)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\ell(\\phi).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Now\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(\\phi,\\phi)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "|\\nabla\\phi|^2\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\pi^2}{2},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\phi)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "\\phi\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{4}{\\pi^2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Hence\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "U\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{8}{\\pi^4},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "u_1(x,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{8}{\\pi^4}\n",
        "\\sin(\\pi x)\\sin(\\pi y).\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is already a numerical approximation of the Poisson problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "No mesh has been used.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Galerkin as a projection\n",
        "\n",
        "The exact weak solution satisfies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(u,v)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\ell(v)\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\forall v\\in V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In particular, since\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V_N\\subset V,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "we have\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(u,v_N)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\ell(v_N)\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\forall v_N\\in V_N.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The Galerkin solution satisfies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(u_N,v_N)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\ell(v_N)\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\forall v_N\\in V_N.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Subtracting the two equations,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "a(u-u_N,v_N)=0\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\forall v_N\\in V_N.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is **Galerkin orthogonality**.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Energy inner product\n",
        "\n",
        "For the Poisson problem,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(v,w)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla w\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "defines an inner product on \\(H_0^1(\\Omega)\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "The associated energy norm is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\|v\\|_a\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sqrt{a(v,v)}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\left(\n",
        "\\int_\\Omega\n",
        "|\\nabla v|^2\\,d\\Omega\n",
        "\\right)^{1/2}.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Galerkin orthogonality therefore means\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u-u_N\n",
        "\\perp_a\n",
        "V_N.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus \\(u_N\\) is the orthogonal projection of \\(u\\) onto \\(V_N\\) with respect to the energy inner product.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Best-approximation property\n",
        "\n",
        "Take any\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "v_N\\in V_N.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Write\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u-v_N\n",
        "=\n",
        "(u-u_N)\n",
        "+\n",
        "(u_N-v_N).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_N-v_N\\in V_N,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Galerkin orthogonality gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(u-u_N,u_N-v_N)=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(u-v_N,u-v_N)\n",
        "=\n",
        "a(u-u_N,u-u_N)\n",
        "+\n",
        "a(u_N-v_N,u_N-v_N).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Hence\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "a(u-u_N,u-u_N)\n",
        "\\le\n",
        "a(u-v_N,u-v_N)\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\forall v_N\\in V_N.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\|u-u_N\\|_a\n",
        "\\le\n",
        "\\|u-v_N\\|_a\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\forall v_N\\in V_N.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "or\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\|u-u_N\\|_a\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\min_{v_N\\in V_N}\n",
        "\\|u-v_N\\|_a.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Interpretation:** among all functions available in the approximation space, the Galerkin solution is the one closest to the exact solution in the energy norm.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the projection interpretation of Galerkin's method.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Relation to Céa's lemma\n",
        "\n",
        "For the symmetric Poisson problem, the result above is an exact best-approximation property.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a more general continuous and coercive bilinear form,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|a(u,v)|\n",
        "\\le\n",
        "M\\|u\\|_V\\|v\\|_V,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a(v,v)\n",
        "\\ge\n",
        "\\alpha\\|v\\|_V^2,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Céa's lemma gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\|u-u_N\\|_V\n",
        "\\le\n",
        "\\frac{M}{\\alpha}\n",
        "\\inf_{v_N\\in V_N}\n",
        "\\|u-v_N\\|_V.\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For the present Poisson problem, the energy inner product makes the projection interpretation particularly simple.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Why finite elements?\n",
        "\n",
        "At this point, Galerkin gives us a powerful principle:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\text{Choose a suitable finite-dimensional space }V_N,\n",
        "\\text{ and Galerkin gives its best approximation.}\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The remaining question is therefore:\n",
        "\n",
        "> **How should we construct \\(V_N\\)?**\n",
        "\n",
        "For the square, analytical functions such as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sin(m\\pi x)\\sin(n\\pi y)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "are natural.\n",
        "\n",
        "But consider:\n",
        "\n",
        "- an L-shaped domain;\n",
        "- a domain containing holes;\n",
        "- a curved mechanical component;\n",
        "- a geometry that changes frequently.\n",
        "\n",
        "Constructing global analytical basis functions satisfying the boundary conditions becomes difficult.\n",
        "\n",
        "Finite differences have a related difficulty: simple Cartesian stencils are naturally adapted to regular grids, but become less convenient near irregular boundaries.\n",
        "\n",
        "Finite elements solve both problems by introducing a mesh.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead of constructing global functions adapted to the whole domain,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\text{divide the domain into simple elements and construct the approximation locally.}\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A finite-element approximation still has the Galerkin form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_h\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_i U_i\\phi_i,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "but the basis functions \\(\\phi_i\\) are generated from the mesh and typically have local support.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Global Galerkin basis | Finite-element basis |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| chosen a priori | generated from a mesh |\n",
        "| adapted to the complete domain | built locally from simple elements |\n",
        "| typically global support | local support |\n",
        "| Dirichlet BCs built into analytical functions | Dirichlet BCs imposed through boundary DOFs |\n",
        "| generic matrix may be dense | matrix is sparse |\n",
        "| convenient for simple geometries | convenient for complex geometries |\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Take-home message**\n",
        ">\n",
        "> Finite elements do not replace the Galerkin principle.\n",
        "> They provide a systematic, local, mesh-based construction of the Galerkin approximation space.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Session 1 in one diagram\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\begin{array}{ccccc}\n",
        "&& -\\Delta u=f &&\\\\[2mm]\n",
        "& \\swarrow && \\searrow &\\\\[-1mm]\n",
        "\\text{approximate derivatives}\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\text{weak formulation}\\\\\n",
        "\\downarrow\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\downarrow\\\\\n",
        "\\text{finite differences}\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\text{Galerkin}\\\\\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\downarrow\\\\\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\text{projection / best approximation}\\\\\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\downarrow\\\\\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\text{choice of approximation space}\\\\\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\downarrow\\\\\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\text{mesh-based local basis}\\\\\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\downarrow\\\\\n",
        "&&&\n",
        "\\text{finite elements}\n",
        "\\end{array}\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# What comes next?\n",
        "\n",
        "Session 2 will answer the practical question:\n",
        "\n",
        "> How does a mesh generate the approximation space \\(V_h\\)?\n",
        "\n",
        "We will introduce:\n",
        "\n",
        "- one-dimensional meshes;\n",
        "- nodal shape functions;\n",
        "- element stiffness matrices;\n",
        "- assembly;\n",
        "- Dirichlet boundary conditions;\n",
        "- triangular \\(P_1\\) elements in two dimensions."
      ],
      "id": "6a8514e1"
    }
